observer (0.6.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The token of a remote machine no longer belongs in machines.json, and one
    written there is refused even when it is the right one. It now lives in
    the Windows Credential Manager, or on Linux in a file readable only by
    its owner, whose permissions are checked every time it is read.
  * The reason changed under it: since that token also authorises ending
    processes on the other machine, a file meant to be opened, copied and
    pasted is the wrong place for it.
  * New command: "observer token set NAME". It reads the token from standard
    input, never from the command line, and does not echo it while it is
    typed.
  * The "name" of a machine is now required, because it is the key its token
    is stored under, and it is validated before it is ever used to build a
    path.
  * Upgrading: run "observer token set NAME" for each remote machine, then
    delete the "apiToken" line from machines.json. Until you do, that machine
    is listed as unusable with the reason.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000

observer (0.5.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Clicking the CPU or the memory gauge opens the list of the fifteen
    processes consuming most of it, refreshed once a second while the panel
    is open, and the selected one can be ended from there.
  * Ending a process is the first thing this service does that is not a read.
    It is allowed over the network with the token, by explicit choice, and
    every attempt - successful or refused by the operating system - is written
    to the log with the pid, the name, and where the caller came from.
  * The process list is deliberately not offered for the disk gauges. Space
    used on a volume cannot be attributed to a running process, and
    per-process traffic needs counters that are not portable and are not
    written yet.
  * Hovering a bar of the history strip now says which minute it covers, and
    says "not measured" on a gap rather than leaving it to be read from the
    hatching.
  * The line under the title now reads "Last Reading: HH:MM:SS". Which machine
    is being watched is already on screen, selected, in the list on the left.
  * A dashboard talking to a service older than itself now says so, instead of
    reporting a 404 it does not know how to interpret.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 14:00:00 +0000

observer (0.4.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * The request timeout rises from 6 to 8 seconds. Six was the first
    attempt and the margin was too thin: a refused connection reached
    through a dual-stack host name costs about 4.1 seconds, and on a busy
    machine that overran the budget - which turns a stopped service back
    into "no answer".
  * The value of each gauge is written below the dial instead of inside it.
    It used to sit just above the hub, which is exactly where the needle
    passes while it moves - so the number was hardest to read at the moment
    it was changing.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 11:00:00 +0000

observer (0.4.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New metric: disk activity. Bytes read and written per second, and the
    percentage of time each device spent working, with one gauge per disk.
  * That percentage is computed from idle time, never by adding read time
    and write time together: those two overlap, and on one measured window
    their sum reached 843%.
  * The instances here are devices - "Disk 0", "sda" - and deliberately do
    not line up with the volume letters used for disk space. A disk carries
    several volumes and a volume can span disks, so attributing the traffic
    of two volumes to one letter would be worse than an honest device name.
  * A refused connection and a silent one are no longer described with the
    same words. They ask for opposite remedies - start a service, or open a
    port - and telling them apart wrongly costs an afternoon.
  * The request timeout rises from 3 to 6 seconds, and the number is
    measured rather than chosen. On Windows a refused connection takes about
    two seconds, and a host name that resolves to two address families pays
    that twice: with the old budget the refusal never arrived in time, and a
    stopped service was reported as unreachable.
  * A certificate that no longer matches its pinned fingerprint now says so
    in the title of the status bar. It used to read "Reading failed", and it
    is the one failure here that should not be answered by retrying.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Mon, 31 Aug 2026 09:00:00 +0000

observer (0.3.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * New metric: disk space, one gauge per mounted volume. It is the first
    per-instance source, so a machine with three drives now shows three
    gauges instead of one number.
  * A volume that reports a size of zero is not reported as empty: the
    capacity is unknown, and saying 0% would be the most reassuring possible
    way to be wrong. It says so, on that volume only.
  * Read and write activity is deliberately not included yet, and neither is
    the SSD/spinning distinction: the latter was measured to lie, reporting
    spinning disks inside a virtual machine whose backing store is NVMe.
  * The service now queries its metric sources together instead of one after
    another. In sequence a round lasted the sum of the timeouts, and a round
    longer than the sampling period drops a sample silently - which downstream
    is indistinguishable from a machine that was switched off.
  * The heading no longer says where the token came from. That belongs in the
    message shown when a fingerprint does not match, where it tells you which
    file to correct, and not in a line reread at every glance.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 19:00:00 +0000

observer (0.2.1) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Windows: the installer now carries the .NET runtime with it. Until 0.2.0 it
    installed binaries that required ASP.NET Core 10 without checking it was
    there: on a machine with an older runtime the service died on startup and
    the installer reported "insufficient privileges", which named the wrong
    cause entirely. The package is now 51 MB instead of 12.8 MB and installs
    with no prerequisites.
  * Linux is unchanged and stays framework-dependent: the package declares
    aspnetcore-runtime-10.0 and apt refuses to install without it, so the
    runtime keeps being updated by the system.
  * Note for Windows: since the runtime now ships inside the package, its
    security updates no longer arrive through Windows Update. They arrive with
    a new release of Observer.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 17:30:00 +0000

observer (0.2.0) unstable; urgency=medium

  * CPU and memory usage are now shown on dashboard gauges, with a needle, a
    graduated arc and a redline, instead of progress bars. Values that are not a
    fraction of a maximum stay written out, under "Usage".
  * New "Last hour" strip under the gauges: one bar per minute, drawn from the
    history the service has been recording all along. Each bar shows the average
    of the minute, with a translucent extension up to the peak it reached.
  * Periods when the service was not running are drawn as gaps, with a dashed
    marker and a broken baseline - never as low usage. A machine that was off is
    not a machine that was idle, and the strip has to say which one it was.
  * Released packages now carry a GitHub build provenance attestation, which
    ties each file to the commit and workflow that produced it. Verify with:
    gh attestation verify Observer.msi --repo Akr0n/observer
  * The release notes no longer conflate the two warnings Windows shows. A
    signature removes "Unknown publisher" immediately; it does not remove the
    SmartScreen warning, which depends on downloads and not on the certificate.
  * "Available memory is an estimate" is no longer a row of its own. The
    note now appears on the value it qualifies, and only when that value
    really is an estimate.

 -- Federico Cardinali <noreply@example.invalid>  Fri, 28 Aug 2026 16:00:00 +0000

